# Neo Keys parameters (docs/specs/neo-keys.md). The order changes only with a version (machines.md §3.2): songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoKeys
namespace NeoBuzz.Keys
class NeoKeysMachine
tracks 16
summary Electric keys by physical modelling: a hammer strikes a tine, a steel reed or a string, and a pickup reads it. The tine and the reed are decaying beam modes beside a slower tone bar or body; the string is a stiff string with a damper. The pickup is the instrument's voice: a magnetic one turns the displacement into a bark that fades as the note dies, an electrostatic one gives the reed an odd-order buzz, and the string has a neck and a bridge pickup. Hardness, touch and a velocity curve shape the hammer; key noise adds the hammer and damper thumps; a preamp drive, a tone tilt and a tremolo with auto-pan finish it. 16 voices and a sustain pedal. Use it for ballad and soul keys, barking drive keys, reedy buzz, funky plucks and muted staccato.

group Model
Model       "Model"         0 2     0     enum Tine Reed String | The instrument: a struck tine read by a magnetic pickup, a struck reed read by an electrostatic one, or a struck string with a damper read by two magnetic pickups
    how Chooses what the hammer strikes and how it is read: a tine beside a tone bar, a steel reed, or a string.
    sound The tine is bell-like and sweet with a bark when played hard, the reed is shorter and buzzier, the string is bright and plucky.
group Hammer
Hardness    "Hardness"      0 1000  380   exp 6 0.15 ms 2                 | The hammer's contact time with the tine, reed or string, 6 ms (soft felt) down to 0.15 ms (hard): a short contact excites the high modes, a long one only the low
    how Sets how hard the hammer is, from soft felt that touches for 6 ms to a hard hammer that touches for 0.15 ms.
    sound A soft hammer gives a round, mellow tone; a hard hammer adds a bright, metallic ping above the note.
Touch       "Touch"         0 1000  600   pct                             | How much the velocity of a note also changes the hammer's contact time: at 100 % a hard key press (velocity 127) is about 6 times shorter, and a soft press 6 times longer, than the Hardness setting
    how Sets how much a harder key press also makes the hammer harder, from 0 % (velocity only changes the level) to 100 %.
    sound Soft playing is dull and quiet and hard playing is bright and loud, as on a real keyboard.
VelCurve    "Velocity curve" 0 1000 500   bip                             | The curve from key velocity to hammer speed: speed is velocity to the power two to the setting, so 0 % is linear, +100 % needs a hard press (velocity squared) and -100 % answers to a light touch (square root)
    how Bends the response of the keys, from -100 % (a light touch plays loud) through 0 % (linear) to +100 % (only a hard press plays loud).
    sound A soft curve makes the keys easy to play loud, a hard curve gives a wide range from whisper to bark.
group Pickup
Pickup      "Pickup"        0 1000  350   pct                             | How close the pickup sits to the vibrating tine, reed or string: at 0 % it is far and reads the motion cleanly, at 100 % it is so close that the response curves and the note barks or growls
    how Moves the pickup from far away (0 %, clean) to almost touching the tine (100 %, bark and growl).
    sound Far is a clean, sine-like bell; close adds a bark when you play hard that smooths out as the note dies away.
Bar         "Bar and body"  0 1000  500   pct                             | The level of the slower partial next to the note: the tone bar beside a tine, the reed's body, or the string's second polarisation, ringing about two to three times longer than the main mode
    how Sets how loud the slow partial is, from 0 % (the tine, reed or string alone) to 100 %.
    sound More bar gives a long, singing sustain under the quick attack; none gives a dry, short pluck.
PickupSel   "Pickup select" 0 2     2     enum Neck Bridge Both           | String only: which magnetic pickup is read, the neck one (full, round), the bridge one (thin, twangy) or both added
    how Chooses the neck pickup, the bridge pickup or both for the String model; the other models ignore it.
    sound The neck pickup is warm and round, the bridge pickup is thin and sharp, both together have notches in between.
group Decay
Decay       "Decay"         0 1000  600   time 0.1 20                     | Decay time of the fundamental, the time it takes to fall by 60 dB (T60); the higher modes die sooner
    how Sets how long the note's fundamental rings, as the time to fall by 60 dB, from 0.1 to 20 s.
    sound Short times are dry and percussive, long times let the notes sing on.
Release     "Release"       0 1000  520   exp 8 1500 ms 0                 | The damper: how fast a released note is damped, the time for it to fall by 60 dB after the key goes up
    how Sets how quickly letting go of a key stops the sound, from 8 ms (a hard damper) to 1.5 s.
    sound A short release is detached and percussive, a long one lets notes ring on after the key goes up.
Mute        "Mute"          0 1    0      switch                          | String only: a muted string, the decay shortened to about an eighth and the higher partials damped much faster, for staccato
    how Switches the string's mute on, shortening its decay and damping its high partials.
    sound Muted notes are short, dry and percussive, like a string stopped with the palm of a hand.
group Noise
KeyNoise    "Key noise"     0 1000  250   pct                             | The level of the mechanical noise of the keyboard: the thump of the hammer at the start of a note and of the damper when the key goes up
    how Sets how loud the hammer and damper thumps are, from 0 % (silent) to 100 %.
    sound A little makes the keys feel real; a lot gives a clunky, woody action.
group Preamp
Drive       "Drive"         0 1000  0     db 0 30                         | The preamp: gain into a soft saturation, 0 dB is clean (the stage is bypassed), up to 30 dB for a compressed, warm overdrive
    how Sets the gain into the preamp's soft saturation from 0 dB (clean) to 30 dB.
    sound More drive thickens and warms the sound and adds odd harmonics, then crunches.
Tone        "Tone"          0 1000  500   bip                             | A tilt around 1 kHz: -100 % lowers the highs by 6 dB and raises the lows by 6 dB, +100 % does the opposite
    how Tilts the balance between low and high, from -100 % (dark) to +100 % (bright).
    sound Down it is round and woolly, up it is thin and glassy.
group Tremolo
TremRate    "Tremolo rate"  0 1000  600   exp 0.5 12 Hz 2                 | Rate of the tremolo, the level swing of the instrument's vibrato circuit
    how Sets the speed of the tremolo from 0.5 to 12 Hz.
    sound Slow is a gentle swell, fast is a flutter.
TremDepth   "Tremolo depth" 0 1000  0     pct                             | Depth of the tremolo: at 100 % the level swings between full and silent
    how Sets how deeply the level swings, from 0 % (off) to 100 %.
    sound Turn it up for the pulsing of a keyboard with its tremolo on.
Stereo      "Auto-pan"      0 1000  0     pct                             | How much of the tremolo is a pan: at 0 % both channels swing together, at 100 % one channel is loud as the other is quiet, so the sound travels left and right
    how Sets how much the tremolo moves the sound between left and right, from 0 % (level only) to 100 % (a full auto-pan).
    sound At 100 % the keys sweep across the stereo field in time with the tremolo.
group Output
Volume      "Volume"        0 1000  790   db -60 12 inf                   | Output level
    how Sets the final level in dB, from off to +12.
    sound It makes the instrument louder or quieter without changing its character.
